From 35978fbb8f290f421d9553526ad0f45c6e381ac9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: RK3128 reference <reference@example.invalid>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 18:40:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] media: verisilicon: g1: Add a JPEG decoder, enable it on
 RK3128

The Hantro G1 decodes baseline JPEG, but mainline only drives the H1's
JPEG encoder. STMicroelectronics wrote a G1 JPEG decoder for its
STM32MP25 tree (Hugues Fruchet, commit 32014c7e991c ("media:
verisilicon: g1: add jpeg decoder support") in
github.com/STMicroelectronics/linux v6.18-stm32mp, with its
restart-interval, unaligned-height and timeout follow-ups); it is not
upstream. Port it to mainline and enable it on
the RK3128 VPU (VDPU1), the SoC's only hardware JPEG decoder. Its
register programming matches Rockchip's own driver for this block (MPP
hal_jpegd_vdpu1), which settled the points below.

The decoder is stateful: a whole JPEG file per OUTPUT buffer
(V4L2_PIX_FMT_JPEG, no controls, no requests), headers parsed in the
kernel with v4l2-jpeg, 4:2:0 written as NV12 and 4:2:2 as NV16 at the
macroblock-aligned size userspace set on OUTPUT. Progressive, 12-bit,
greyscale, 4:4:4, 4:1:1 and non-interleaved JPEGs are refused (the
buffer comes back with the error flag). On RK3128 NV16 is listed only
while the OUTPUT format is JPEG, and a JPEG context offers no
post-processed formats, so the other codecs see the formats they did;
the frame size goes up to the datasheet's 8176x8176.

Changes from ST's file:
- A job the hardware cannot take ends through hantro_irq_done(), as the
  VPU981 AV1 decoder's does. ST returned an error to device_run(),
  which keeps the job's runtime PM reference and clocks (on RK3128 the
  reference holds the bus shared with the HEVC decoder) and leaves the
  armed watchdog to abort a later job.
- Huffman tables of any size: an optimised table has fewer than 162
  AC or 12 DC values, and the slots of the values it lacks stay zero.
  ST took only the standard sizes.
- The quantisation table of each component is the one its frame
  header selects, the hardware's first Huffman table of each class is
  the luma's, and a chroma component selects the second one when its
  table is another (ST set the chroma selectors from the stream's
  table numbers even when the luma table was swapped into the first
  slot). Tables nothing uses may be missing.
- The stream length counts from the 64-bit word holding the first byte
  of the entropy-coded segment, the address the hardware is given.
- The bottom fill (and the height8 flag) only for 4:2:2 with 1 to 8
  lines in the last macroblock row, whose lower half is not coded; a
  4:2:0 MCU is a whole macroblock. The restart interval register is
  written for every job (the H.264 decoder uses it too).
- The OUTPUT buffers of a JPEG context get a kernel mapping, chosen in
  queue_setup from the format, which cannot switch between JPEG and
  another codec while buffers exist; other decoders keep none.

A JPEG job does not run while a decoder context of another codec on the
same device is streaming: it comes back with the error flag at once. A
video has priority over a photo, and a 12 MP JPEG holds the core for
about 130 ms on RK3128, several frames of a video.

Originally-by: Hugues Fruchet <hugues.fruchet@foss.st.com>
Signed-off-by: RK3128 reference <reference@example.invalid>
